{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+2y-z}{(x-z)^{2}},\\, & y(0)=4 \\\\ {z}^{′}=xy+2z,\\, & z(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and $z(2).$\n\n$\\,y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-0=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x+2y-z}{(x-z)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+2\\cdot 4-2}{(0-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+8-2}{(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xy+2z \\\\  & =0\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  | $2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+3=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+8=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |  |  | $10$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈7$\nand\n$z(2)≈10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-333886" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "