d $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-y+2z \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-3+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-3+2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x+y+z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3+1 \\\\  & =2+3+1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ |  | $1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+2=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+4=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+12=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $13$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈7$\nand\n$z(3)≈13.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-333890" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333890" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333890" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333890" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "13"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=4x+y-z,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x+4y+z,\\, & z(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=2?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z(2)≈$\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=0,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+y-z \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0+1-0 \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+4y+z \\\\  & =0+4\\cdot 1+0 \\\\  & =0+4+0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $0$ | $4$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+2=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+8=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈3$ and $z(2)≈8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333813" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=6x+2y+z,\\, & y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2x-y+z,\\, & z(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z(2)≈$\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=3,$ we place $y=-1$ and $z=3$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6x+2y+z \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+3 \\\\  & =6-2+3 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x-y+z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-(-1)+3 \\\\  & =2+1+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $7$ |  | $3$ | $6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+7=6, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3+6=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $6$ |  |  | $9$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈6$ and $z(2)≈9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333823" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=3x+2y+z,\\, & y(0)=3 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2x-y+3z,\\, & z(0)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=2?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z(2)≈$\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=3$ and $z(0)=-2,$ we place $y=3$ and $z=-2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x+2y+z \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3+(-2) \\\\  & =0+6-2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x-y+3z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-3+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0-3-6 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $4$ |  | $-2$ | $-9$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-9)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $-2$ | $-9$ | $-18$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+8=11 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+(-18)=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $-2$ | $-9$ | $-18$ |\n| $2$ | $11$ |  |  | $-20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈11$\nand\n$z(2)≈-20.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-18"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333827" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-20"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x-y,\\, & y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=1+y,\\, & z(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=0$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0$ and $z(0)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y=0-0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =1+y=1+0=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =1+y=1+0=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(1)≈1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-335132" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335132" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335132" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335132" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2x+y-z,\\, & y(-1)=2 \\\\ {z}^{′}={x}^{2}-y+2z,\\, & z(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=-1$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=2$ and $z(-1)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+y-z=2\\cdot (-1)+2-0=0, \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-y+2z=(-1)^{2}-2+2\\cdot 0=-1, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-1)\\cdot 1=-1, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+y-z=2\\cdot 0+2-(-1)=3, \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-y+2z={0}^{2}-2+2\\cdot (-1)=-4, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-4)\\cdot 1=-4, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+3=5, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+(-4)=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ |  |  | $-5$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈5$\nand\n$z(1)≈-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-333277" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333277" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333277" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333277" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=1-x+2y+z,\\, & y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x-y+{z}^{2},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3)$ and $z(3).$\n\n$y(3)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(3)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1-x+2y+z=1-1+2\\cdot (-1)+1=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+{z}^{2}=1-(-1)+{1}^{2}=3, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-1)\\cdot 1=-1, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-1)=-2, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+3=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $-2$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1-x+2y+z=1-2+2\\cdot (-2)+4=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+{z}^{2}=2-(-2)+{4}^{2}=20, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-1)\\cdot 1=-1, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=20\\cdot 1=20, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+(-1)=-3, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+20=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $20$ | $20$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ |  |  | $24$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-3$\nand\n$z(3)≈24.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-333279" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333279" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333279" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-333279" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=y+z,\\, & y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=1-x,\\, & z(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=0$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0$ and $z(0)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y+z=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =1-x=1-0=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y+z=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =1-x=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(1)≈\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-335125" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335125" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335125" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335125" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-332892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x+3y-{z}^{2},\\, & y(-2)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x-y+2z,\\, & z(-2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0).$\n\n$y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-332892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=-2$ and asked to find their values at $x=0.$ The distance from $x=-2$ to $x=0$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-2)}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-2)=1$ and $z(-2)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+3y-{z}^{2}=-2+3\\cdot 1-{1}^{2}=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2z=-2-1+2\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-2+1=-1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+3y-{z}^{2}=-1+3\\cdot 1-{0}^{2}=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2z=-1-1+2\\cdot 0=-2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-2\\cdot 1=-2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+2=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+(-2)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n| $0$ | $3$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(0)≈3$\nand\n$z(0)≈-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-332892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-332892" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-332892" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-332892" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-332892" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=xz+1,\\, & y\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=y+w-x,\\, & z\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=-yz,\\, & w\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(0),$ $z(0),$ and $w(0).$\n\n$\\,y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(0)≈$\n{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0-(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xz+1 \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =y+w-x \\\\  & =0+2-(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {w}^{′} & =-yz \\\\  & =-(0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{9}{4}$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈\\frac{1}{4},\\,z(0)≈\\frac{9}{4},\\,w(0)≈2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-335285" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335285" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335285" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335285" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335285" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335285" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\sqrt[3]{x+y-1}+z,\\, & y(0)=2 \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-1}{w+1},\\, & z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=\\sqrt{x+{z}^{2}}-y,\\, & w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$, $z$ and $w$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[3]{x+y-1}+z \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{0+2-1}+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-1}{w+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}-1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {w}^{′} & =\\sqrt{x+{z}^{2}}-y \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+{1}^{2}}-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ |  | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $4$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈4,$\n$z(1)≈\\frac{5}{2},$\nand\n$w(1)≈0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-335367" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335367" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335367" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335367" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335367" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335367" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x+y+{w}^{2},\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2y-z,\\, & z(0)=2 \\\\ {w}^{′}=-w+xz,\\, & w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(1)≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z(1)≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w(1)≈$\n{{field-6}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=2,$ and $w(0)=1,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=2,$ and $w=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y+{w}^{2} \\\\  & =0+1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2y-z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =-w+xz \\\\  & =-1+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $0$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-1)\\cdot 1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+2=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+0=2 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈3,$\n$z(1)≈2,$\nand\n$w(1)≈0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-335155" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335155" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335155" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335155" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944