le_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335155" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335155" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=y+z+w,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=xy-{w}^{2},\\, & z(0)=-2 \\\\ {w}^{′}=\\frac{yz}{w},\\, & w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ $z\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and $w\\,(\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n$\\,y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y+z+w \\\\  & =1+(-2)+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xy-{w}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =\\frac{yz}{w} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-2)}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $-2$ | $-1$ |  | $1$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈1,\\,z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈-\\frac{5}{2},\\,w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-335290" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335290" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335290" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335290" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335290" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335290" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=1-2y+z,\\, & y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2+x-w,\\, & z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=yz,\\, & w(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-6}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=0,$ $z(0)=1,$ and $w(0)=2,$ we place $y=0,$ $z=1,$ and $w=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1-2y+z \\\\  & =1-2\\cdot 0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2+x-w \\\\  & =2+0-2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =yz \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |  | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+0=1 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈1,$\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈1,$\nand\n$w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a295938de2a14bb19f5ce48b0116269b9ea6f265635118ca5147870715a4763"} {"question" "q-335310" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335310" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335310" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335310" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "v