eld" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335310" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "q-335310" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/state.json" "file_sha256" "ccf05f032472648c57e68697a846d83a98b387944041e9743ccad72dd697656b"} {"question" "e-21655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x){:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=4x+5y-z,\\, & y(0)=3 \\\\ {z}^{′}=5x-4y+2z,\\, & z(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/example-21655.json" "file_sha256" "d01454586ca49f0c97cbe57024362f44558e92df46c378ed5093f8764d3c4eb7"} {"question" "e-21655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and $z_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+5y-z \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 3-4 \\\\  & =0+15-4 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =5x-4y+2z \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =0-12+8 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ |  | $4$ | $-4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =11\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ | $11$ | $4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+11=14 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+(-4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ | $11$ | $4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n| $1$ | $14$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1) \\approx 14$\nand\n$z(1) \\approx 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/example-21655.json" "file_sha256" "d01454586ca49f0c97cbe57024362f44558e92df46c378ed5093f8764d3c4eb7"} {"question" "e-21656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x){:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x-y+2z,\\, & y(1)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=3x+y-z,\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3)$ and $z(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/example-21656.json" "file_sha256" "f572930c09041e4e75b5930fec3cd9ece3ba16d69e5cf19ada855be503a3b558"} {"question" "e-21656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and $z_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = \\dfrac22 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y+2z=1-1+2\\cdot 0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3x+y-z=3\\cdot 1+1-0=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+4=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y+2z=2-1+2\\cdot 4=9 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3x+y-z=3\\cdot 2+1-4=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=9\\cdot 1=9 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+9=10 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $9$ | $9$ | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $10$ |  |  | $7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3) \\approx 10$\nand\n$z(3) \\approx 7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/example-21656.json" "file_sha256" "f572930c09041e4e75b5930fec3cd9ece3ba16d69e5cf19ada855be503a3b558"} {"question" "e-21660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x), w=w(x){:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2y+z+x,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=y+z+2x,\\, & z(0)=0 \\\\ {w}^{′}=wy-x,\\, & w(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproximate values of $y$, $z$ and $w$ at $x = 1$ using Euler's method if the step size is $\\Delta x = 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/example-21660.json" "file_sha256" "26d169e6ddfd3a958a6dce75ff40c4e8a89899706dbea62d06da5899424c5db7"} {"question" "e-21660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=0,$ and $w(0)=2,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=0,$ and $w=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y', z',$ and $w'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y+z+x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+0+0 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =y+z+2x \\\\  & =1+0+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =wy-x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $0$ | $1$ |  | $2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y,$ $\\Delta z,$ and $\\Delta w$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ and $\\Delta w$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+2=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+1=1 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3},$\n$z(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1},$\nand\n$w(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044404/example-21660.json" "file_sha256" "26d169e6ddfd3a958a6dce75ff40c4e8a89899706dbea62d06da5899424c5db7"}] "usage" {"e-21660" [] "q-333813" [] "e-21655" [] "e-21656" [] "q-332892" []} "basis" 1654 "retractions" [[17592186167179 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x)$:\n${y^{′} = 4x + y - z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(0) = 1 \\\\ z^{′} = x + 4y + z,\\end{vmatrix}z(0) = 0$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx = 2$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | $z^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2) \\approx$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z(2) \\approx$\n{{field-4}}."] [17592186167164 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x)$:\n${y^{′} = x + 3y - z^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}y(-2) = 1 \\\\ z^{′} = x - y + 2z,\\end{vmatrix}z(-2) = 1$\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0)$.$y(0) \\approx$\n{{field-1}},\n$z(0) \\approx$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186166480 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x){:}$\n${y^{′} = 4x + 5y - z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(0) = 3 \\\\ z^{′} = 5x - 4y + 2z,\\end{vmatrix}z(0) = 4$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1)$."] [17592186166480 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = f(x, y, z),\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = g(x, y, z),\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{Δy = y^{′} \\cdot Δx,; Δz = z^{′} \\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z\n$$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $3$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 4x + 5y - z \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 3 - 4 \\\\\n&= 0 + 15 - 4 \\\\\n&= 11 \\\\\nz^{′} &= 5x - 4y + 2z \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 0 - 4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 0 - 12 + 8 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ |  | $4$ | $-4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 11 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 11 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (-4) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ | $11$ | $4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$, $\\Delta y$ to $y$, and $\\Delta z$ to $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 3 + 11 = 14 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz = 4 + (-4) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ | $11$ | $4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n| $1$ | $14$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 14\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz(1) \\approx 0\n$$"] [17592186166481 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x){:}$\n${y^{′} = x - y + 2z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(1) = 1 \\\\ z^{′} = 3x + y - z,\\end{vmatrix}z(1) = 0$\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3)$ and $z(3)$."] [17592186166481 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = f(x, y, z),\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = g(x, y, z),\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{Δy = y^{′} \\cdot Δx,; Δz = z^{′} \\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z\n$$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3$. The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2$, and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = \\dfrac22 = 1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=0$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y$, $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new}$, $y_\\text{new}$, and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x - y + 2z = 1 - 1 + 2 \\cdot 0 = 0 \\\\\nz^{′} &= 3x + y - z = 3 \\cdot 1 + 1 - 0 = 4 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 0 \\cdot 1 = 0 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + 0 = 1 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz = 0 + 4 = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y$, $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new}$, $y_\\text{new}$, and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x - y + 2z = 2 - 1 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 9 \\\\\nz^{′} &= 3x + y - z = 3 \\cdot 2 + 1 - 4 = 3 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 9 \\cdot 1 = 9 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx = 3 \\cdot 1 = 3 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 2 + 1 = 3 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + 9 = 10 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz = 4 + 3 = 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $9$ | $9$ | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $10$ |  |  | $7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(3) \\approx 10\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz(3) \\approx 7\n$$"] [17592186166485 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x), w=w(x){:}$\n${y^{′} = 2y + z + x,\\begin{vmatrix}y(0) = 1 \\\\ z^{′} = y + z + 2x,\\end{vmatrix}z(0) = 0; w^{′} = wy - x, \\mid w(0) = 2$\n\nApproximate values of $y$, $z$ and $w$ at $x = 1$ using Euler's method if the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$."] [17592186166485 :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$, $z(0)=0$, and $w(0)=2$, we place $y=1$, $z=0$, and $w=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y', z'$, and $w'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2y + z + x \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 1 + 0 + 0 \\\\\n&= 2 \\\\\nz^{′} &= y + z + 2x \\\\\n&= 1 + 0 + 2 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 1 \\\\\nw^{′} &= wy - x \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $0$ | $1$ |  | $2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$, $\\Delta z$, and $\\Delta w$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx = 1 \\cdot 1 = 1 \\\\\nΔw &= w^{′} \\cdot Δx = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x$, $y$, $z$, and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$, $\\Delta y$ to $y$, $\\Delta z$ to $z$, and $\\Delta w$ to $w$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + 2 = 3 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz = 0 + 1 = 1 \\\\\nw_{new} &= w + Δw = 2 + 2 = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3}\n$$\n\n$$\nz(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nw(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{4}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [[17592186164391 :answer-field/id] [17592186164391 :answer-field/key] [17592186164391 :answer-field/type] [17592186164392 :answer-field/id] [17592186164392 :answer-field/key] [17592186164392 :answer-field/type] [17592186164482 :answer-field/id] [17592186164482 :answer-field/key] [17592186164482 :answer-field/type] [17592186164483 :answer-field/id] [17592186164483 :answer-field/key] [17592186164483 :answer-field/